IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Family Provision Act 1972 (Western Australia) -- who may claim provision out of a dead person's estate (s 7(1), (2A),
-- read with the definitions in s 4(1) and the parentage rule in s 4(4), (4a)), the time for claiming (s 7(2), (3)), what
-- the Supreme Court may then order (s 6(1)-(4)), a second claim for property that was not disclosed (s 6(5), (7)), an
-- interim order for immediate needs (s 7A), who bears the provision (s 14(2)), dealing with provision before the order
-- (s 19), and the claimant's notice to the administrator (s 20(4), (5)).
--
-- SEAT: the family member left out of a will, or left too little by it or by the intestacy rules. Every decision is phrased
-- as that person would ask it: "May I claim against the estate?", "Am I in time?", "Can the Court order provision for me?",
-- "Can the will shut out my claim?". Whether the will or intestacy fails to make adequate provision for the claimant's
-- "proper maintenance, support, education or advancement in life" is the Court's opinion (s 6(1)); it is asked as a fact,
-- never guessed. So are the Court's leave to file out of time, its refusal on the ground of character or conduct, and the
-- other judgments the Act hands to it.
--
-- Provisions covered: s 4(1) ("administrator", "child", "grandchild", "stepchild", "will"), s 4(4), (4a), s 6(1)-(5), (7),
-- (8), s 7A(1), s 7(1)(a)-(e), (2A), (2), (3), s 12(1) (one comment), s 14(2), s 19, s 20(4), (5), s 21B (the prescribed
-- amount, as an input).
--
-- Edition: compilation from legislation.wa.gov.au (mrdoc 49104), as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49104.txt, retrieved 7 September 2026. The deposit names no compilation number; its
-- compilation table ends with the Statutes (Minor Amendments) Act 2017 s 10 (in force 13 September 2017); the last reprint
-- listed is Reprint 2 as at 22 February 2013; the uncommenced provisions table lists the Evidence Act 2025 s 492(1) and the
-- Assisted Reproductive Technology and Surrogacy Act 2025 Pt 17 Div 7 (text not shown, not encoded). No regulation is
-- deposited: the "prescribed amount" for s 7(1)(eb), which s 21B lets the Governor prescribe, is an input.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: ss 1-3, 5, 6A (title, commencement, repeal of the Testator's Family Maintenance Act 1939, transitional
-- application, Crown bound -- every death here is after 1973); s 6(6) (whom a second claim must be served on); s 7A(2);
-- ss 8, 9 (an order under the Trustees Act 1962 s 65 once the estate is distributed); s 10 (an order takes effect as a
-- codicil or as a modification of the intestacy rules -- noted, not computed); s 11 (urgent distributions); s 12(2) (one
-- application treated as made for all); s 13 (class fund); s 14(1), (3)-(7) (form of order, settled property, costs);
-- ss 15-17 (rescission, increase, commutation); s 18 (replacing an administrator); s 20(1)-(3) (the administrator's own
-- protection, except as it touches the claimant through s 20(4), (5)); s 21A (evidence of the deceased's statements);
-- s 21 (rules of court). Who takes on intestacy is the Administration Act 1903; whether a will is valid is the Wills Act
-- 1970; whether a jointly owned home or a superannuation benefit is part of "the estate" at all is not decided by this Act
-- (it never defines "estate").
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the
-- individual to whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Family Provision Act 1972 (WA)`

§§ `numbers and dates the Act turns on`

-- s 7(2)(a): "within 6 months from the date on which the administrator becomes entitled to administer the estate";
-- s 6(7)(a): "within 6 months from the date on which the applicant became aware of the existence of the undisclosed property".
`the months allowed to apply` MEANS 6

-- s 20(5): "three months after the date on which he first receives notice ... or ... 12 months from the date on which the
-- administrator became entitled to administer the estate".
`the months a notice of intention lasts` MEANS 3
`the months from the grant after which any notice lapses` MEANS 12

-- s 7(2A): stepchildren may not apply "unless the deceased person died on or after the day on which the Inheritance (Family
-- and Dependants Provision) Amendment Act 2011 section 9(1)(b) came into operation". Compilation table: that Act's Pt 2
-- "16 Jan 2013 (see s. 2(b) ...)". That s 9(1)(b) is in Pt 2 and came into operation with it is an INFERENCE from the table.
`the day stepchildren could first claim` MEANS Date 16 1 2013

-- Other notes, note 2: Acts Amendment (Lesbian and Gay Law Reform) Act 2002 s 82 -- an application for provision out of the
-- estate of a person "who died before the commencement of this Part is to be dealt with under that Act as if this Part had
-- not commenced". Compilation table: Pt 13 in force "21 Sep 2002". That Part amended ss 4 and 7 (No. 3 of 2002 ss 80, 81);
-- the text it replaced is not in the deposit.
`the day the 2002 reforms commenced` MEANS Date 21 9 2002

GIVEN `a` IS A DATE
      `b` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is on or after` MEANS Day `a` AT LEAST Day `b`

-- "N months from" a date is read as the same day of the month N months later (INFERENCE: the Interpretation Act 1984
-- reading of a period of months is not deposited). A day that does not exist in the later month rolls into the next.
GIVEN `the start` IS A DATE
      `n` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`months from` MEANS Date (DATE_DAY `the start`) (DATE_MONTH `the start` PLUS `n`) (DATE_YEAR `the start`)

-- `the earlier of` two dates is the daydate library's.

§§ `4, 7(1) -- the facts I can state about myself`

-- s 7(1): "(a) a person who was married to, or living as the de facto partner of, the deceased person immediately before the
-- death ...; (b) a person who at the date of the death ... was receiving or entitled to receive maintenance from the deceased
-- as a former spouse or former de facto partner ...; (c) a child of the deceased ...; (d) a grandchild of the deceased ...;
-- (ea), (eb) a stepchild of the deceased ...; (e) a parent of the deceased ...".
-- s 4(1): "child ... includes an illegitimate child"; "grandchild ... includes an illegitimate child of a child".
-- "Married" and "de facto partner" are not defined in this Act: borrowed terms, asked as facts.
DECLARE Relationship IS ONE OF
    `married to the deceased when the deceased died`              -- s 7(1)(a)
    `the deceased's de facto partner when the deceased died`      -- s 7(1)(a)
    `formerly married to the deceased`                             -- s 7(1)(b)
    `formerly the deceased's de facto partner`                     -- s 7(1)(b)
    `the deceased's child`                                         -- s 7(1)(c)
    `the deceased's grandchild`                                    -- s 7(1)(d)
    `a child of the deceased's spouse or de facto partner, not the deceased's child` -- s 4(1) "stepchild", s 7(1)(ea), (eb)
    `the deceased's parent`                                        -- s 7(1)(e)
    `another relative, a friend or a charity`                      -- not in s 7(1): a brother, a niece, a foundation

-- s 7(1)(c), (d)(ii), (iii): "living at the date of the death of the deceased, or born within 10 months after the deceased's
-- death".
DECLARE `When I was born` IS ONE OF
    `before the deceased died`
    `within 10 months after the deceased died`
    `more than 10 months after the deceased died`

DECLARE `My position` HAS
    `my relationship to the deceased`                    IS A Relationship
    `when I was born`                                    IS A `When I was born`
    `the deceased was maintaining me wholly or partly immediately before the death` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 7(1)(b) "receiving", (d)(i), (ea)
    `I was entitled to be maintained by the deceased at the death` IS A BOOLEAN       -- s 7(1)(b) "entitled to receive", (ea)
    `my parent, a child of the deceased, died before the deceased` IS A BOOLEAN       -- s 7(1)(d)(ii), (iii)
    `I was alive when the deceased married or began a de facto relationship with my parent` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 4(1) "stepchild"
    `the deceased took property from my parent's estate, not as a creditor` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 7(1)(eb)(i)
    `the value of that property at my parent's death`    IS A NUMBER                  -- s 7(1)(eb)(ii), in dollars
    `the parentage I rely on was admitted or established in the parent's lifetime` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 4(4)
    `the parentage I rely on arises under the Artificial Conception Act 1985` IS A BOOLEAN    -- s 4(4a)
    `as the parent, my parentage was admitted by the deceased as an adult or established in the deceased's lifetime` IS A BOOLEAN -- s 7(1)(e)

§§ `4(1) -- am I a stepchild`

-- s 4(1): "stepchild means a person who was alive on the date on which the deceased married or entered into a de facto
-- relationship with a parent of the person but who is not a child of the deceased".
GIVEN `me` IS A `My position`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `am I the deceased's stepchild` IF
    CONSIDER `me`'s `my relationship to the deceased`
    WHEN `a child of the deceased's spouse or de facto partner, not the deceased's child` THEN
        `me`'s `I was alive when the deceased married or began a de facto relationship with my parent`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `me` IS A `My position`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`maintained or entitled to be` MEANS
        `me`'s `the deceased was maintaining me wholly or partly immediately before the death`
    OR  `me`'s `I was entitled to be maintained by the deceased at the death`

GIVEN `me` IS A `My position`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`born by 10 months after the death` MEANS
    CONSIDER `me`'s `when I was born`
    WHEN `more than 10 months after the deceased died` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `7(1) -- the classes of person who may apply`

-- s 7(1)(a): "married to, or living as the de facto partner of, the deceased person immediately before the death".
GIVEN `me` IS A `My position`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`limb (a)` MEANS
    CONSIDER `me`'s `my relationship to the deceased`
    WHEN `married to the deceased when the deceased died` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the deceased's de facto partner when the deceased died` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 7(1)(b): "receiving or entitled to receive maintenance from the deceased as a former spouse or former de facto partner of
-- the deceased whether pursuant to an order of any court, or to an agreement or otherwise".
GIVEN `me` IS A `My position`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`limb (b)` MEANS
    CONSIDER `me`'s `my relationship to the deceased`
    WHEN `formerly married to the deceased` THEN `maintained or entitled to be` `me`
    WHEN `formerly the deceased's de facto partner` THEN `maintained or entitled to be` `me`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 7(1)(c): "a child of the deceased living at the date of the death of the deceased, or born within 10 months after the
-- deceased's death". No age limit, and no need to have been dependent.
GIVEN `me` IS A `My position`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`limb (c)` MEANS
    CONSIDER `me`'s `my relationship to the deceased`
    WHEN `the deceased's child` THEN `born by 10 months after the death` `me`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 7(1)(d): a grandchild "(i) who was being maintained wholly or partly by the deceased immediately before the deceased's
-- death; or (ii) who, at the date of the deceased's death, was living and one of whose parents was a child of the deceased
-- who had predeceased the deceased; or (iii) who was born within 10 months after the deceased's death and one of whose
-- parents was a child of the deceased who had predeceased the deceased". (i) requires actual maintenance; a bare
-- entitlement is not enough.
GIVEN `me` IS A `My position`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`limb (d)` MEANS
    CONSIDER `me`'s `my relationship to the deceased`
    WHEN `the deceased's grandchild` THEN
            `me`'s `the deceased was maintaining me wholly or partly immediately before the death`
        OR  (`born by 10 months after the death` `me` AND `me`'s `my parent, a child of the deceased, died before the deceased`)
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 7(1)(ea): "a stepchild of the deceased who was being maintained wholly or partly or was entitled to be maintained wholly
-- or partly by the deceased immediately before the deceased's death".
GIVEN `me` IS A `My position`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`limb (ea)` MEANS `am I the deceased's stepchild` `me` AND `maintained or entitled to be` `me`

-- s 7(1)(eb): "a stepchild of the deceased, if -- (i) the deceased received or was entitled to receive property from the
-- estate of a parent of the stepchild, otherwise than as a creditor of that estate; and (ii) the value of that property, at
-- the time of the parent's death, is greater than the prescribed amount". s 21B: the amount is prescribed by regulation;
-- none is deposited, so it is an input.
GIVEN `me` IS A `My position`
      `the prescribed amount` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`limb (eb)` MEANS
        `am I the deceased's stepchild` `me`
    AND `me`'s `the deceased took property from my parent's estate, not as a creditor`
    AND `me`'s `the value of that property at my parent's death` GREATER THAN `the prescribed amount`

-- s 7(1)(e): "a parent of the deceased, whether the relationship is determined through a legal marriage or otherwise, where
-- the relationship was admitted by the deceased being of full age or established in the lifetime of the deceased".
GIVEN `me` IS A `My position`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`limb (e)` MEANS
    CONSIDER `me`'s `my relationship to the deceased`
    WHEN `the deceased's parent` THEN
        `me`'s `as the parent, my parentage was admitted by the deceased as an adult or established in the deceased's lifetime`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `4(4), (4a) -- parentage that counts`

-- s 4(4): "other than for the purposes of section 7(1)(e), the relationship between a parent and a child and any other
-- relationship traced in any degree through that relationship, shall be recognised only if parentage is admitted by or
-- established against the parent in the parent's lifetime". s 4(4a): "Subsection (4) does not apply to ... a relationship
-- established by the Artificial Conception Act 1985."
-- Read as reaching a child, a grandchild and a stepchild (the stepchild's tie to the deceased runs through the stepchild's
-- own parent). Applying it to a stepchild is an INFERENCE.
GIVEN `me` IS A `My position`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`do I rely on a parent and child relationship` MEANS
    CONSIDER `me`'s `my relationship to the deceased`
    WHEN `the deceased's child` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the deceased's grandchild` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a child of the deceased's spouse or de facto partner, not the deceased's child` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `me` IS A `My position`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is the parentage I rely on recognised` MEANS
        `me`'s `the parentage I rely on was admitted or established in the parent's lifetime`
    OR  `me`'s `the parentage I rely on arises under the Artificial Conception Act 1985`

GIVEN `me` IS A `My position`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`do I rely on a de facto relationship` MEANS
    CONSIDER `me`'s `my relationship to the deceased`
    WHEN `the deceased's de facto partner when the deceased died` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `formerly the deceased's de facto partner` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `7(1), (2A) -- may I claim against the estate`

DECLARE Standing IS ONE OF
    `yes: I am a person by whom an application may be made (s 7(1))`
    `no: I am not in any class that section 7(1) names`
    `no: the parentage I rely on was not admitted or established in the parent's lifetime (s 4(4))`
    `no: a stepchild may apply only if the deceased died on or after 16 January 2013 (s 7(2A))`
    `not covered: the deceased died before 21 September 2002 and I rely on a de facto relationship; the text that then applied is not in the deposit`

@export May I claim provision out of the estate (Family Provision Act 1972 (WA) s 7(1), (2A))?
GIVEN `me` IS A `My position` @desc Your relationship to the person who died, and the facts s 7(1) turns on
      `the date of death` IS A DATE @desc The date the person died
      `the prescribed amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount prescribed by regulation for s 7(1)(eb), in dollars (not deposited)
GIVETH A Standing
DECIDE `May I claim against the estate` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `do I rely on a de facto relationship` `me` AND (NOT `is on or after` `the date of death` `the day the 2002 reforms commenced`)
            THEN `not covered: the deceased died before 21 September 2002 and I rely on a de facto relationship; the text that then applied is not in the deposit`
        IF `do I rely on a parent and child relationship` `me` AND (NOT `is the parentage I rely on recognised` `me`)
            THEN `no: the parentage I rely on was not admitted or established in the parent's lifetime (s 4(4))`
        IF (`limb (ea)` `me` OR `limb (eb)` `me` `the prescribed amount`) AND (NOT `is on or after` `the date of death` `the day stepchildren could first claim`)
            THEN `no: a stepchild may apply only if the deceased died on or after 16 January 2013 (s 7(2A))`
        IF     `limb (a)` `me` OR `limb (b)` `me` OR `limb (c)` `me` OR `limb (d)` `me`
            OR `limb (ea)` `me` OR `limb (eb)` `me` `the prescribed amount` OR `limb (e)` `me`
            THEN `yes: I am a person by whom an application may be made (s 7(1))`
        OTHERWISE `no: I am not in any class that section 7(1) names`

GIVEN `the standing` IS A Standing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`may apply` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the standing`
    WHEN `yes: I am a person by whom an application may be made (s 7(1))` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `6(1), (2) -- can the will or the intestacy shut me out`

-- s 6(1): the Court acts where "the disposition of the deceased's estate effected by his will, or the law relating to
-- intestacy, or the combination of his will and that law" does not make adequate provision. s 6(2): the Court "shall not be
-- bound to assume that the law relating to intestacy makes adequate provision in all cases". Nothing in the Act gives effect
-- to a clause in a will that forbids or excludes a claim; the will is the thing s 6(1) measures. So a will cannot shut a
-- claim out, and neither can the fact that the estate passed on intestacy.
@export Can the will, or the estate passing under the intestacy rules, shut out my claim (s 6(1), (2))?
GIVEN `the will says I am to have nothing and make no claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will excludes you or forbids a claim
      `the estate passed wholly or partly on intestacy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether some or all of the estate passed under the intestacy rules
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the will or the intestacy shut out my claim` IF FALSE

§§ `7(2), (3) -- am I in time`

-- s 7(2): "No application under subsection (1) shall be heard by the Court unless -- (a) the application is made within
-- 6 months from the date on which the administrator becomes entitled to administer the estate of the deceased in Western
-- Australia; or (b) the Court is satisfied that the justice of the case requires that the applicant be given leave to file
-- out of time." s 7(3): "A motion for leave to file out of time may be made at any time".
-- s 4(1): "administrator means any person to whom probate of the will ... is granted, or to whom administration is granted",
-- and the Public Trustee once it files an election to administer. So the clock starts at the grant (or the election).
-- s 12(1): notice of the application "shall be served by the applicant on the administrator".
@export What is the last day I can apply without the Court's leave (s 7(2)(a))?
GIVEN `the date the administrator became entitled to administer the estate in WA` IS A DATE @desc The date of the grant of probate or administration in Western Australia (or the Public Trustee's election)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `What is the last day I can apply without leave` IS
    `months from` `the date the administrator became entitled to administer the estate in WA` `the months allowed to apply`

DECLARE Timing IS ONE OF
    `in time: made within 6 months from the grant (s 7(2)(a))`
    `out of time, but it will be heard: the Court has given leave to file out of time (s 7(2)(b))`
    `out of time: it will not be heard unless the Court is satisfied the justice of the case requires leave, which I may seek at any time (s 7(2)(b), (3))`

@export Will my application be heard, or is it out of time (s 7(2), (3))?
GIVEN `the date the administrator became entitled to administer the estate in WA` IS A DATE @desc The date of the grant of probate or administration in Western Australia
      `the date I file my application` IS A DATE @desc The date you file your application in the Supreme Court
      `the Court is satisfied the justice of the case requires leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has given you leave to file out of time
GIVETH A Timing
DECIDE `Is my application in time` IS
    BRANCH
        IF Day `the date I file my application`
               AT MOST Day (`What is the last day I can apply without leave` `the date the administrator became entitled to administer the estate in WA`)
            THEN `in time: made within 6 months from the grant (s 7(2)(a))`
        IF `the Court is satisfied the justice of the case requires leave`
            THEN `out of time, but it will be heard: the Court has given leave to file out of time (s 7(2)(b))`
        OTHERWISE `out of time: it will not be heard unless the Court is satisfied the justice of the case requires leave, which I may seek at any time (s 7(2)(b), (3))`

GIVEN `the timing` IS A Timing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`will be heard` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the timing`
    WHEN `out of time: it will not be heard unless the Court is satisfied the justice of the case requires leave, which I may seek at any time (s 7(2)(b), (3))` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `6(1), (3), (4) -- can the Court order provision for me`

-- s 6(1): "if the Court is of the opinion that the disposition ... is not such as to make adequate provision from his estate
-- for the proper maintenance, support, education or advancement in life of any of the persons mentioned in section 7 ...
-- the Court may, at its discretion, on application made by or on behalf of any such person, order that such provision as
-- the Court thinks fit is made out of the estate".
-- s 6(3): "The Court may attach such conditions to the order as it thinks fit, or may refuse to make an order in favour of
-- any person on the ground that his character or conduct is such as in the opinion of the Court to disentitle him to the
-- benefit of an order, or on any other ground which the Court thinks sufficient."
-- s 6(4): "the provision may consist of a lump sum or a periodical or other payment".
-- s 10: the provision takes effect as if made by a codicil executed immediately before the death, or as a modification of
-- the intestacy rules.
-- The adequacy of provision is the Court's opinion and the order is its discretion: both are asked as facts.
DECLARE Outcome IS ONE OF
    `the Court may order such provision for me as it thinks fit, as a lump sum or a periodical or other payment, with conditions (s 6(1), (3), (4))`
    `no order: I am not a person who may apply (s 7(1))`
    `no order: no application has been made by me or on my behalf (s 6(1))`
    `no order: my application will not be heard, being out of time without leave (s 7(2))`
    `no order: the Court is not of the opinion that adequate provision was not made for my proper maintenance, support, education or advancement in life (s 6(1))`
    `no order: the Court refuses because of my character or conduct, or on another ground it thinks sufficient (s 6(3))`

@export Can the Supreme Court order provision for me out of the estate (s 6(1), (3), (4))?
GIVEN `my standing` IS A Standing @desc The answer to "May I claim against the estate?"
      `an application has been made by me or on my behalf` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (or someone for you) have applied to the Supreme Court
      `my timing` IS A Timing @desc The answer to "Will my application be heard?"
      `the Court is of the opinion that adequate provision was not made for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is of the opinion that the will or intestacy does not make adequate provision for your proper maintenance, support, education or advancement in life
      `the Court refuses on my character or conduct or another sufficient ground` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court refuses an order on the ground of your character or conduct, or another ground it thinks sufficient
GIVETH AN Outcome
DECIDE `Can the Court order provision for me` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `may apply` `my standing` THEN `no order: I am not a person who may apply (s 7(1))`
        IF NOT `an application has been made by me or on my behalf` THEN `no order: no application has been made by me or on my behalf (s 6(1))`
        IF NOT `will be heard` `my timing` THEN `no order: my application will not be heard, being out of time without leave (s 7(2))`
        IF NOT `the Court is of the opinion that adequate provision was not made for me`
            THEN `no order: the Court is not of the opinion that adequate provision was not made for my proper maintenance, support, education or advancement in life (s 6(1))`
        IF `the Court refuses on my character or conduct or another sufficient ground`
            THEN `no order: the Court refuses because of my character or conduct, or on another ground it thinks sufficient (s 6(3))`
        OTHERWISE `the Court may order such provision for me as it thinks fit, as a lump sum or a periodical or other payment, with conditions (s 6(1), (3), (4))`

§§ `7A -- an interim order for my immediate needs`

-- s 7A(1): "Before making an order under section 6(1), the Court may make an interim order if it is of the opinion that such
-- an order is necessary for the purpose of providing those things immediately necessary for the maintenance, support or
-- education ... of any person who was totally or partially dependent on the deceased immediately before the deceased's
-- death."
@export May the Court make an interim order for my immediate needs before it decides my claim (s 7A)?
GIVEN `an application under section 6(1) is before the Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a claim for provision has been made and is undecided
      `I was totally or partially dependent on the deceased immediately before the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you depended on the deceased, wholly or in part, just before the death
      `the Court is of the opinion an interim order is necessary for my immediate needs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court thinks an interim order is necessary for things immediately needed for your maintenance, support or education
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the Court make an interim order for me` IF
        `an application under section 6(1) is before the Court`
    AND `I was totally or partially dependent on the deceased immediately before the death`
    AND `the Court is of the opinion an interim order is necessary for my immediate needs`

§§ `6(5), (7), (8) -- a further claim for property that was not disclosed`

-- s 6(5): where an order "has been made or could have been made" for a person, a further order may be made on that person's
-- application "only if ... (a) the evidence about the nature and extent of the estate did not reveal the existence of
-- certain property (the undisclosed property); and (b) the value of the undisclosed property would have materially affected
-- the provision". s 6(7): not heard unless "(a) the application is made within 6 months from the date on which the
-- applicant became aware of the existence of the undisclosed property; or (b) the Court is satisfied that the justice of
-- the case requires" leave. s 6(8): leave may be sought at any time. Whether the value would have "materially affected" the
-- provision is the Court's judgment: asked as a fact.
@export May I bring a further claim because property of the estate was not disclosed (s 6(5), (7))?
GIVEN `an order was or could have been made for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an order was made, or could have been made, for you on your first claim
      `the evidence did not reveal the property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence about the estate failed to reveal the property
      `the Court finds its value would have materially affected my provision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court finds the property's value would have materially affected your provision
      `the date I became aware of the property` IS A DATE @desc When you became aware the property existed
      `the date I file` IS A DATE @desc When you file the further application
      `the Court gives leave to file out of time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court gives leave because the justice of the case requires it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I bring a further claim for undisclosed property` IF
        `an order was or could have been made for me`
    AND `the evidence did not reveal the property`
    AND `the Court finds its value would have materially affected my provision`
    AND (    Day `the date I file` AT MOST Day (`months from` `the date I became aware of the property` `the months allowed to apply`)
         OR  `the Court gives leave to file out of time`)

§§ `14(2) -- whose share pays for my provision`

-- s 14(2): "Subject to subsection (3), and unless the Court otherwise orders, the burden of any provision shall, as between
-- the persons beneficially entitled to the estate of the deceased, be borne by those persons in proportion to the value of
-- their respective interests in such estate." The answer is the default; the Court may order otherwise, and s 14(3) (settled
-- property charged against corpus) is not encoded.
@export How much of the provision ordered for me falls on one beneficiary's share, unless the Court orders otherwise (s 14(2))?
GIVEN `the provision ordered for me` IS A NUMBER @desc The provision ordered for you, in dollars
      `the value of that beneficiary's interest` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of that beneficiary's interest in the estate, in dollars
      `the value of all beneficial interests in the estate` IS A NUMBER @desc The total value of all beneficiaries' interests, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much of my provision falls on that share` IS
    IF `the value of all beneficial interests in the estate` EQUALS 0
    THEN 0
    ELSE `the provision ordered for me` TIMES `the value of that beneficiary's interest` DIVIDED BY `the value of all beneficial interests in the estate`

§§ `19 -- may I borrow against my provision before the order`

-- s 19: "No mortgage, charge, or assignment of any kind whatsoever which is given of or over any provision out of the estate
-- of any deceased person granted by any order of the Court under this Act and which is given before the order of the Court
-- is made shall be of any force, validity or effect."
@export Is a mortgage, charge or assignment I give over my provision of no effect under s 19?
GIVEN `I gave it before the Court made the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the mortgage, charge or assignment before the order was made
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my dealing with my provision of no effect` IF `I gave it before the Court made the order`

§§ `20(4), (5) -- my notice to the administrator, and what I give up by consenting`

-- s 20(5): "Notice to an administrator of an intended application shall lapse and shall be incapable of being renewed, and
-- the administrator may act as if he had not received the notice, if, before the expiration of three months after the date
-- on which he first receives notice of the intention to make the application or before the sooner expiration of 12 months
-- from the date on which the administrator became entitled to administer the estate ..., the administrator does not receive
-- notice that the application has been made to the Court; but nothing in this subsection shall prevent the subsequent making
-- of the application." The notice protects me only until the earlier of the two dates, unless by then I have applied.
@export If I tell the administrator I intend to claim, on what date does my notice lapse unless I have applied by then (s 20(5))?
GIVEN `the date the administrator first received my notice` IS A DATE @desc When the administrator first received notice that you intend to apply
      `the date the administrator became entitled to administer the estate in WA` IS A DATE @desc The date of the grant in Western Australia
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `When does my notice of intention lapse` IS
    `the earlier of`
        (`months from` `the date the administrator first received my notice` `the months a notice of intention lasts`)
        (`months from` `the date the administrator became entitled to administer the estate in WA` `the months from the grant after which any notice lapses`)

-- s 20(4): a person entitled to apply "who, being of full legal capacity, advises the administrator in writing that he --
-- (a) consents to a proposed distribution; or (b) does not intend to make any application under this Act that would affect
-- a proposed distribution, shall not bring an action against the administrator by reason of his having thereafter
-- distributed any part of the estate". It bars an action against the administrator; it does not on its words bar a claim
-- for provision against what remains.
@export Have I lost the right to sue the administrator for a later distribution, by consenting in writing (s 20(4))?
GIVEN `I advised the administrator in writing that I consent, or that I will not apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you told the administrator in writing that you consent to the proposed distribution or will not claim
      `I was of full legal capacity when I did` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were of full legal capacity at the time
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Have I lost my action against the administrator` IF
    `I advised the administrator in writing that I consent, or that I will not apply` AND `I was of full legal capacity when I did`
